/*!
  \page install_macos Building on macOS (community-supported)
  macOS is not one of the \ref install_supportedos "officially supported operating systems" for PandA/Bambu.
  It is possible to build the framework natively on macOS (as opposed to using the \ref install_docker "Docker image"),
  but doing so currently requires one important workaround described below. This page assumes you are otherwise
  following the generic \ref install_source "build from source" instructions, using a Homebrew-provided LLVM/Clang
  toolchain (e.g. `llvm@16`) as the frontend compiler.

  \section install_macos_ar Apple ar/ranlib silently drop non-Mach-O archive members

  \subsection install_macos_ar_symptom Symptom
  A native macOS build completes without any configure or compile errors, but `bambu` later fails during HLS synthesis
  with errors such as:
  \verbatim
  error -> The library miss this function __float_mul (called_function)
  \endverbatim
  or similar "library miss this function" errors for other runtime helpers (soft-float, libm, pthread, ...), even
  though the corresponding source file (e.g. `etc/libbambu/softfloat/softfloat.c`) is present and was compiled
  without error.

  \subsection install_macos_ar_cause Cause
  The `etc/libbambu` resource libraries (`libbambu`, `libm`, `libsoftfloat`, `soft-fp`, `pthread`, ...) are not meant
  to be linked by a native linker. Their `.o` members are produced by `tree-panda-gcc` as gzip-compressed LLVM
  bitcode wrapped to look like object files, and are parsed directly by Bambu itself at HLS time - they are never
  valid Mach-O objects.

  The `ar`/`ranlib` shipped with Xcode Command Line Tools (`/usr/bin/ar`, `/usr/bin/ranlib`) silently drop any
  archive member they do not recognize as Mach-O when the archive's symbol table is (re)built, instead of storing it
  as an opaque member the way GNU/LLVM `ar` does. The result is a `.a` file that contains only the `__.SYMDEF SORTED`
  symbol table and none of the actual objects - i.e. an effectively empty runtime library - which is why Bambu can't
  find e.g. `__float_mul` at synthesis time.

  You can confirm this is happening by inspecting one of the built archives, e.g.:
  \verbatim
  ar t <prefix>/lib/panda/libsoftfloat_clang16.a
  \endverbatim
  If this prints only `__.SYMDEF SORTED` (no `.o` member listed), the archive is empty. During the build you will
  also see warnings such as:
  \verbatim
  ranlib: warning: archive member 'libsoftfloat_clang16_a-softfloat.o' not a mach-o file
  \endverbatim
  These warnings are easy to miss/ignore in the middle of a long build log, but they are the actual root cause.

  \subsection install_macos_ar_fix Fix
  Use LLVM's `ar`/`ranlib` instead of Apple's. They ship alongside the Clang/LLVM toolchain (e.g. via
  `brew install llvm@16`, under `$(brew --prefix llvm@16)/bin/llvm-ar` and `llvm-ranlib`) and store any file as an
  archive member regardless of its format, matching the behavior PandA's build expects on Linux.

  Most of the `etc/libbambu` archives are built through Automake's standard archiving rules, which honor `$(AR)`/
  `$(RANLIB)` and so can simply be overridden on the `make` command line:
  \verbatim
  make AR=llvm-ar RANLIB=llvm-ranlib
  make AR=llvm-ar RANLIB=llvm-ranlib install
  \endverbatim

  `libbambu_clang16.a` is the one exception: it is produced by `tree-panda-gcc`'s own archive-merging code path
  (see `command = "ar cru " + ...` in `src/tree-panda-gcc.cpp`), which shells out to a hardcoded `ar` rather than
  respecting `$(AR)`. Overriding `AR=` on the `make` command line has no effect on this step. To fix it too, make
  sure `ar`/`ranlib` resolve to the LLVM versions via `$PATH` for the duration of the build, for example by placing
  symlinks ahead of `/usr/bin` in `$PATH`:
  \verbatim
  mkdir -p /tmp/llvm-ar-shim
  ln -sf "$(brew --prefix llvm@16)/bin/llvm-ar"     /tmp/llvm-ar-shim/ar
  ln -sf "$(brew --prefix llvm@16)/bin/llvm-ranlib" /tmp/llvm-ar-shim/ranlib
  PATH="/tmp/llvm-ar-shim:$PATH" make AR=llvm-ar RANLIB=llvm-ranlib
  PATH="/tmp/llvm-ar-shim:$PATH" make AR=llvm-ar RANLIB=llvm-ranlib install
  \endverbatim

  If you already ran a build/install before applying this workaround, the stale empty archives under your build
  directory and install prefix will not be regenerated just by re-running `make` (the `.a` target is already
  up to date as far as `make` is concerned). Delete them first so the archiving rules re-run:
  \verbatim
  find <builddir>/etc/libbambu -name '*.a' -delete
  \endverbatim
  then rebuild/install as shown above. Verify the fix with the same `ar t` check from
  \ref install_macos_ar_symptom "the symptom section" above - you should now see the real `.o` member(s) listed.

  \section install_macos_xml_tb Native Verilator simulation of an XML-testbench build fails with x86-only flags or static linking

  \subsection install_macos_xml_tb_symptom Symptom
  Using the older, self-contained testbench flow (`--generate-tb=<file.xml>` together with `--simulate
  --simulator=VERILATOR`, as opposed to the newer `--generate-tb=<file.c>` DPI/co-simulation flow -- see
  \ref install_macos_xml_tb_vs_dpi "the note below" on the difference) fails at one of several points with
  errors such as:
  \verbatim
  error: unknown FP unit 'sse'
  ...
  ld: library 'crt0.o' not found
  clang++: error: linker command failed with exit code 1
  \endverbatim
  or the native reference-value executable fails to compile/link with `'stdio.h' file not found` or
  `ld: library 'm' not found`.

  \subsection install_macos_xml_tb_cause Cause
  Two source locations unconditionally pass x86-only flags that a non-x86 host's compiler/linker rejects
  outright, with no Darwin (or non-x86) carve-out:
  - `src/HLS/simulation/CTestbenchExecution.cpp` and `src/HLS/simulation/testbench_values_c_generation.cpp`
    both hard-code `-msse2 -mfpmath=sse` (selecting an x86 floating-point unit) into the flags used to compile
    the native reference executable that computes expected values for the testbench.
  - `src/wrapper/simulation/verilator/VerilatorWrapper.cpp` hard-codes `-LDFLAGS -static` for the Verilator
    build; macOS has no static libSystem/crt0.o, so fully static linking is never possible there (the same
    reason `configure.ac` already sets `panda_ALLSTATIC=no` under `TARGET_DARWIN`).

  Neither of these was hit by the more commonly-exercised DPI/co-simulation testbench flow (`--generate-tb=
  <file.c>`), which goes through an entirely different code path (`src/wrapper/compiler/compiler_wrapper.cpp`) --
  that is why they weren't caught by earlier Darwin porting work.

  Separately (not a bug, just an environment requirement): unlike compiling bambu itself, *running* it also
  needs a discoverable SDK sysroot, because bambu shells out to the frontend compiler again at simulation time
  to build the native reference executable. `SDKROOT` (see \ref install_macos_ar_fix "the ar/ranlib fix above"
  for how to obtain it) only supplies header search paths, not library paths, so the native executable's link
  step additionally needs `-L<sdkroot>/usr/lib` supplied explicitly via `--testbench-extra-gcc-flags`.

  \subsection install_macos_xml_tb_fix Fix
  Both hard-coded flag additions were changed to `#if !defined(__APPLE__)`, matching the existing convention
  already used elsewhere in `testbench_values_c_generation.cpp` (e.g. its handling of `-Wl,--allow-multiple-
  definition`, which Apple's linker also does not support).

  With that fix in place, a full run on macOS needs `SDKROOT` exported and an explicit SDK library path passed
  through to the native reference-value build, for example:
  \verbatim
  export SDKROOT="$(xcrun --show-sdk-path)"
  bambu --generate-tb=test.xml --simulate --simulator=VERILATOR --verilator-parallel \
        --testbench-extra-gcc-flags="-L${SDKROOT}/usr/lib" \
        ... (other flags) ...
  \endverbatim

  \subsection install_macos_xml_tb_vs_dpi Note: --generate-tb=<file.c> vs --generate-tb=<file.xml>
  These are two unrelated testbench mechanisms, not two spellings of the same feature:
  - `--generate-tb=<file.xml>` (the only form documented in `bambu --help` at this revision) supplies concrete
    input values for the top function's formal parameters directly, via a `<function><testbench PARAM="{...}"
    .../></function>` XML document (see `examples/mm_float/test.xml` for a worked example). It has been part
    of bambu since long before this branch point and needs no DPI runtime at all -- the entire flow (frontend
    HLS, RTL generation, native reference-value computation, and Verilator simulation) runs as one self-
    contained process tree.
  - `--generate-tb=<file.c>` (a C driver source file, e.g. the pattern used by
    `examples/c-to-verilog/*/forward_kernel_testbench.c` in soda-benchmarks) is a newer capability that
    delegates stimulus, memory contents, and correctness checking to a DPI-C-imported co-simulation runtime
    (`libmdpi`) talking to a *separate* driver process. See `verilator-sst`'s `MDPI_TESTBENCH_POC.md` for the
    (non-deterministic, unresolved) issues hit trying to reuse that flow standalone.

  When pointed at a `.xml` argument value, `--generate-tb`'s argument parser only recognizes the option as an
  XML path if it ends in literally `.xml`; anything else (including a `.c` file path) silently falls through to
  a completely different branch that expects an inline `key=value[,key=value]*` string, and fails with the
  unrelated-looking `Error in processing --simulate arg` (`src/HLS/simulation/test_vector_parser.cpp`) when it
  cannot find an `=` in what it's given.

  One more XML-format detail worth knowing: a `<testbench>` attribute name must match the *post-preprocessing*
  formal parameter name, not necessarily the name as written in the function's declaration. A common bambu
  idiom is to declare `#pragma HLS_interface P0 m_axi direct` per pointer argument alongside a `#define
  <descriptive_name> P0`-style macro (see `examples/c-to-verilog/3mm-v2/forward_kernel.c` in soda-benchmarks)
  so the source reads naturally (`A`, `B`, `C`, ...) while every occurrence -- including the parameter name
  itself -- expands to `P0`, `P1`, etc. before bambu ever parses it. The XML must use those expanded names
  (`P0="{...}"`, not `A="{...}"`), or bambu fails late, after a full HLS run, with `Value of P0 is missing in
  test vector`.

*/
